        "text": "1. Your email address. 2. Your password. 3. Sign in. If your account has two factor authentication enabled you are asked for a code next. Recovery codes work here too, and each one is single use. The dashboard: what to work on (0.31) Signing in lands you on the Dashboard . It answers, from stored data, what is broken, which businesses it affects, which devices need attention, what is already being worked on, and what to do next. It covers only what you can see: a Business User sees the devices granted to them, a technician sees their assignment's business and computers, a Platform Admin sees every business. A section you have no permission for is not shown at all — it is never shown as a zero. Part What it is built from Site outage strip An open site outage alert: several devices at one site stopped reporting together Devices Active devices (archived and not yet enrolled ones excluded): online, offline, and agent silent — reachable through RustDesk but the agent has not checked in for 30 minutes, so nothing it reports is current Critical alerts Open critical alerts. Alerts in a maintenance window are counted apart, not as critical Failed actions Approved action and patch targets that failed, were refused or did not run in the last 7 days, plus any whose outcome is unknown (those block new requests until someone records what they found) Awaiting approval Plans waiting for the approval roster. Approval happens on the signer, never here; expired requests are not counted Restart pending Devices whose latest update scan says Windows is waiting for a restart, or that have a restart pending alert. A scan older than 36 hours is flagged stale Patch compliance Of the devices with an update scan under 36 hours old, the share that list no pending updates. Coverage (how many devices have such a scan) and the scan ages are shown beside it; with no fresh scan at all it says No data Active sessions Sessions connected now, and ones still launching. A session row that has not moved for 12 hours is not shown as live Work on next A ranked list, each item with the reason it is there (below) Customers affected Businesses with an outage, an open alert, an offline device or a failed action Devices needing attention Devices with critical or warning alerts, failed actions, offline, agent silent or restart pending, most serious first Being worked on Live sessions, acknowledged alerts (and who acknowledged them), actions in flight, patch deployments in progress Your recent devices The devices of your own most recent sessions Recent activity Alert, action, patch, session, device and support request events from the audit log, last 7 days. Needs View business audit log How \"Work on next\" is ordered. A fixed ladder, then oldest first: site outages, critical alerts, actions with an unknown outcome, failed actions, warning alerts, failed client rollouts (Platform Admins), approval requests, restarts pending from a fresh scan, then info alerts. Acknowledged alerts are not in it (someone has them), nor are alerts in a maintenance window or offline alerts grouped under a site outage. Nothing is ranked by anything the dashboard cannot see, such as a customer's contract. Every item and figure links to where you act on it — the device's Monitoring, Patches or Actions tab, the patch deployment, the alerts list. The page polls every 30 seconds and says how old its data is. Server health (CPU, memory, disk, relay) is not on the dashboard: Platform Admins find it under Settings → System status . Device locations (the map) are under Show device locations at the bottom."
