    "html": "<h1 id=\"msp-technicians\">MSP technicians<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#msp-technicians\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h1>\n<p><em>Added in v0.20.</em></p>\n<p>An MSP technician supports several customer businesses but belongs to none of them. Until v0.20 the only ways to give such a person access were to make them a Platform Admin (every business, every setting) or a member of one business (one business, nothing else). Technician assignments sit between the two. They give one person, or a group, defined access to one business, optionally to some of its computers, optionally only for a while or during set hours. Every grant is recorded and can be revoked.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-does-what\">Who does what<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#who-does-what\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"docs-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Create or revoke an assignment, manage technician groups</td>\n<td>Platform Admin, or anyone holding the <code>technicians:manage</code> platform permission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Give someone <code>technicians:manage</code></td>\n<td>Platform Admin only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ask for access to a business</td>\n<td>Any current technician</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approve or deny a pending request</td>\n<td>That business&#39;s Business Owner, or a Platform Admin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Require approval before technicians can work in a business</td>\n<td>That business&#39;s Business Owner, or a Platform Admin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A Business Owner can <strong>never create</strong> an assignment. If they could, one business could hand access to itself to an outsider, with no accountability on the MSP side. The owner&#39;s part is approval, and only for their own business.</p>\n<p>Changing assignments, groups or platform permissions needs a <a href=\"/docs/sign-in#step-up\">step-up</a> (<code>technicians.manage</code>). Approving or denying needs <code>permissions.change</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"assignments\">Assignments<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#assignments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An assignment names:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>who</strong>: one person, or a technician group (everyone in the group gets it);</li>\n<li><strong>which business</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>which capabilities</strong>, from the same list a business uses for its own users (<em>Users → Permissions</em>). <code>users:manage</code> cannot be given to a technician;</li>\n<li><strong>which computers</strong>: every computer in the business, or a specific list;</li>\n<li><strong>when</strong>, optionally: a start, an expiry, and a weekly schedule in an explicit time zone, e.g. weekdays 08:00–18:00 America/New_York.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of this is re-evaluated on <strong>every request</strong>, not stored in the session. An assignment that expires, leaves its schedule, or is revoked stops working on the technician&#39;s next click, even mid-session. This matches how a suspended account is handled.</p>\n<p><strong>Presets (v0.23).</strong> An assignment is either created from a preset (L1 Help Desk, L2 Technician, L3 Senior, or a clone) or given explicit capabilities. Asking for neither gives <strong>L1 Help Desk</strong>. A preset-backed assignment&#39;s capabilities are derived live from the preset and never include Remote connect or credential access: sessions come from the launch policy and temporary grants instead. Explicit-capability (custom) assignments, including every assignment made before 0.23, behave as they always did. Capability and computer scope are taken from the <strong>same</strong> assignment, so overlapping grants no longer combine. A manager cannot assign themselves, or a group they belong to, and cannot add themselves to a group. See <a href=\"/docs/technician-access\">technician-access.md</a>.</p>\n<p>A technician acting in a customer business through an assignment is <strong>never a Business Owner there</strong>, whatever role they hold in their own business. They get the assignment&#39;s capabilities and nothing more.</p>\n<h3 id=\"temporary-access\">Temporary access<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#temporary-access\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Give the assignment an expiry. The person&#39;s <em>All assigned</em> page and the computer&#39;s <a href=\"/docs/offboarding#access-review\">access review</a> both show it as temporary, with its end time. When it expires, it moves to the person&#39;s <em>inactive</em> list with the reason <code>expired</code>, so they can see why it vanished.</p>\n<h2 id=\"requests-and-approval\">Requests and approval<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#requests-and-approval\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A technician can ask for access to a business from <strong>All assigned → Request access</strong>. A request must have a reason and an expiry: open-ended access is never self-service.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A request <strong>always</strong> waits as <code>PENDING</code> and grants nothing until that business&#39;s Business Owner (or a Platform Admin) approves it at <strong>Technician approvals</strong>. An approver can narrow a request (fewer capabilities, fewer computers, an earlier expiry) but can never widen it.</li>\n<li><em>Require approval</em> on a business goes further: it makes assignments created by a technician manager wait for the owner&#39;s approval as well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"working-in-a-business\">Working in a business<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#working-in-a-business\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Anyone with at least one live assignment sees a <strong>business switcher</strong> at the top of the sidebar. Choosing a business sets the <code>rem0te_business</code> cookie. The API honours it only for the person&#39;s own business or one they are assigned to at that moment; anything else is silently treated as their own business. The amber <em>Technician access</em> label under the business name shows when the switcher is in that mode.</p>\n<p><strong>All assigned</strong> (<code>/technician</code>) is a read-only overview across every business the person can reach. Each business is queried with <strong>its own</strong> assignment&#39;s scope, so a broad grant in one business never widens what is shown for another.</p>\n<h2 id=\"see-also\">See also<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#see-also\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/access-control\">Access control</a>: the three levels and the business boundary.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/technician-access\">Technician access</a>: presets, the launch policy, protected device classes and fleet-abuse limits.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/offboarding\">Offboarding</a>: removing a technician everywhere at once.</li>\n</ul>\n",
