        "heading": "Starting a session",
        "id": "starting-a-session",
        "text": "Every way of starting a session goes through one decision ( ): , , a connection grant redemption, and a launcher token at issue and at redemption. In order: 1. Paused identity gets nothing. 2. Protected device class (below): denied for everyone. 3. Unattended mode: refused while the policy says off. 4. Business members and Platform Admins: the pre 0.23 decision, unchanged. The requested mode is not applied to them : a view only or elevated request is approved with the permanent password and RustDesk opens with full control. Since 0.25.0 the View only button is offered only in a technician context, so it no longer implies otherwise. 5. Custom technician assignments with on this computer: unchanged. 6. Preset backed technicians: view only needs a preset that allows it on this device class, and an endpoint that reports it can enforce view only and (when consent is required) attended consent; control needs an activated temporary grant for this computer, and consent enforcement; elevation is unavailable. If the endpoint cannot enforce it, the launch is blocked with the reason. It is never silently opened with more. And a preset backed launch never receives the permanent password : it would outlive the grant and allow a direct reconnect the portal never sees. The launch mode is recorded on the grant and the launcher token, and redemption re evaluates that mode , never a wider one. A demotion, revocation, pause or expiry between minting and redemption ends it. What the endpoint can enforce today RustDesk's view only, clipboard and file transfer switches are toggles on the technician's client. The endpoint options that exist apply machine wide, and the shipped installer sets , so the endpoint never asks its user to accept. No shipped agent reports . So in practice: every view only and every consent gated launch is blocked with / ; clipboard and file transfer are Unavailable ; the session length is shown at launch but nothing ends the session ; the endpoint agent can observe that an incoming RustDesk connection ended (its process count reaches zero), but nothing on the endpoint can terminate a session. For everyone who is not a preset technician, the same configuration means the computer accepts its permanent password with nobody asked, so an ordinary Connect is unattended in effect. See security model.md. The test suite simulates an enforcing endpoint by writing directly. None of these restrictions has been live verified against RustDesk."
