        "text": "Control What is enforced, and where Default What it does not prove Signed agent updates (update signing.md) The endpoint agent runs only targets signed offline against its compiled in root. The portal holds no key Legacy allowed , no repository configured Fixture tested only. Legacy agents still trust the portal until migrated Approved actions (approved actions.md) Two catalog actions: start the Print Spooler, and (0.27) install named Windows updates through a staged deployment. The portal only proposes; a separate approval signer signs the exact plan after user verified passkey approvals from roster members who are not the requester (one for a standard workstation, two distinct otherwise, plus customer sign off where required); the agent verifies that signature with keys from the offline rooted chain, its own identity, product type, window and build, and journals the job so it never runs twice. The command channel is otherwise an allowlist: inventory refresh, update scan, event log query Off until an approval signer is configured Fixture tested only. A started spooler is not a printed document. A compromised portal can delay or withhold delivery, but cannot sign Staged Windows patching (staged patching.md) A canary, then rings of up to 25, each one signed plan needing two distinct approvers. The next ring is refused until the canary reports every update effective, the failure threshold holds and the soak has passed; over the threshold the deployment pauses itself. Each computer refuses if WSUS, WUfB policy, MDM or ConfigMgr manages it, and installs only what Windows Update itself offers as a security, critical, rollup or ordinary update. It never restarts or accepts a licence Off until approved actions are, and agents run the 0.27 signed build Fixture tested only, against a simulated Windows Update. Effective is what the computer reports, not proof a vulnerability is fixed. No restarts, no uninstall Containment (containment.md) A global write pause, or a suspension of one business, stops approved actions (and delivery of approved plans), remote connections, agent update flags and ClientSt0r jobs, asked by every write path after access. A business's own suspension is lifted only by its owner; an MSP pause by a second person; lifting needs fresh authentication. The approval signer keeps its own copy that only its CLI lifts, and a signed makes agents refuse whatever the portal delivers Nothing paused A compromised portal ignores its own pause; the signer and signed pause layers hold then. Fixture tested only Tamper evident audit log (containment.md) Every entry is hash chained within seconds, sealed entries are guarded by a database trigger, the chain is verified hourly, and entries can be streamed off the host with Ed25519 signed checkpoints and compared with Sealing and verify on; export and signing off until configured Detects, does not prevent: a database superuser can still delete rows and is caught afterwards. Without an export, a full rewrite of the chain is not detectable Replay protection Connection grants (90 s), launcher tokens (120 s), enrollment tokens and integration nonces are single use by conditional update. Integration nonces are stored in Postgres, so they survive a restart Always on — Fleet limits Per identity caps on distinct computers, businesses, launches and writes. A trip pauses the identity until someone else clears it 60 minute window; see technician access.md Connections made outside the portal are not counted AI assisted ticket analysis (ClientSt0r) (integration clientst0r.md) rem0te authorizes each job itself: a signed request, an explicit mapping, the delegated technician's live access (never Platform Admin), and a fleet limit admission. A job reads bounded evidence. Its output is text only ( ). From 0.26 ClientSt0r may propose the catalog action as the delegated technician; an AI suggestion is labelled as one and gets exactly the same approvals Off until configured A mapped ticket is context, not "
