        "text": "A portal pause is only as trustworthy as the portal. If the portal or its database may be compromised, the other two layers are what hold. Layer Who can set it Who can lift it Holds if the portal is compromised? Portal As above As above No. A compromised portal can ignore its own pause Approval signer The portal (automatically, for any pause covering actions), or the signer operator Only the signer operator , with the signer CLI Yes, for new approvals. The portal can add a pause there but has no route that lifts one Agent: signed pause The release operator, with the offline targets key The release operator, by publishing a pause that pauses nothing Yes. Agents refuse whatever the portal delivers The approval signer While it holds a pause for a business, or a global one, the signer refuses new plans for it ( ) and refuses every approval decision, including one already in progress. Lifting the portal's pause does not lift the signer's copy. Check it, then lift it on the signer host: This needs the 0.28 signer build ( ). An older signer answers the portal's pause request with 404; the portal records that as and its own pause still holds. The signed pause is published through the signed update repository (see Update signing → Publishing a signed pause). An agent on a 0.28+ signed build that finds itself covered: refuses an approved action with , after verifying the plan and before journaling it. The refusal is final, so lifting the pause doesn't revive that job; with paused, refuses the update command before downloading anything. A pause that is listed but can't be read counts as paused. A compromised portal can withhold a newer pause.json only until the signed timestamp expires, after which agents refuse the repository."
