      {
        "heading": "Security",
        "id": "",
        "text": "Rem0te Security Audit — August 2026 Scope: full repository security review focused on multi tenant isolation, authentication, remote access credentials, installer/updater supply chain, and privileged operations. This document records what was fixed, what was verified, and what remains as future work. Two passes are recorded here: the original review, and a second full pass on 2026 08 31 (v0.13.0, Deadbolt ) which is written up first because it found the more serious problems of the two. Three passes are recorded here, newest first: 2026 10 22 (v0.18.22), which covered everything added since 0.13.0; 2026 08 31 (v0.13.0, Deadbolt ); and the original review."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Third pass — 2026-10-22 (v0.18.22)",
        "id": "third-pass-2026-10-22-v01822",
        "text": "Scope: the whole repository again, including what came after 0.13.0 — inventory and the command queue, event log reading, agent reinstall, host metrics and the Tactical RMM resolver. The live deployment's ports, TLS, headers and file permissions were checked too, as were , and Dependabot. Severity Found Fixed : : Critical 3 3 High 3 3 Medium 9 9 Low 12 12 Every finding has a check, either in (it needs a running API against a scratch database, and refuses port 3001) or in . The changelog entry for 0.18.22 gives each one's symptom and the reasoning behind its fix. This is the summary. Severity Finding Fix C1 Critical A device reported hostname reached / lines of the Connect ; ran as the technician reduces name and ID to ; claim DTO bounded C2 Critical on an API writable path → root Root owned downloads, verifies and installs C3 Critical could be pre created by a standard user → SYSTEM via Ownership + SID based ACL + verification before any write; planted items removed; client pinned by digest or signature H1 High A Business Owner could register another business's RustDesk ID and receive its secret and password RustDesk IDs typed in by Platform Admins only H2 High sudoers matches spaces, so the fail2ban rules admitted Root owned with anchored, per operation argument checks H3 High Served Windows installer predated its own source's security fix Rebuilt with Go 1.26.10, which now names M1 Medium Sign in open redirect ( , ) Resolved with , same origin and non protocol relative only M2 Medium TOTP: per IP limit only; replay within the window Per account lockout; single use M3 Medium TOTP setup silently deactivated active MFA; self MFA reset without a code Refused while active; self reset refused M4 Medium bypassed per computer grants for credentials Same reach as connecting; company wide needs M5 Medium Business detail listed computers to members with no capabilities Needs M6 Medium Note attachable to another business's session Session checked against the note's business M7 Medium Computer pointable at another business's site Site checked against the computer's business M8 Medium Claim tokens reusable under a race; pool adoption without the secret Conditional consume; existing secret required M9 Medium Unbound nodes bound by the first heartbeat Only a claim binds; a token minted for that computer can re bind L1–L12 Low No session revocation; unvalidated profile body; email change without re auth; API keys outliving their creator's role; launcher scope, Quick Connect switches and ; heartbeat oracle; DTO less public routes; SVG and name derived extensions on uploads; no console CSP; Next on ; cleartext LAN site; firewall off See the 0.18.22 changelog Not changed, deliberately: keeps , because the App Router inlines its hydration payload and a nonce based CSP would need dynamic rendering of every page. The policy still blocks foreign script origins, plugins, hijacking, framing and off origin form posts and fetches."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Second pass — 2026-08-31 (v0.13.0)",
        "id": "second-pass-2026-08-31-v0130",
        "text": "Scope: every authentication, authorization, crypto, privileged process and unauthenticated surface in , , , , and ; the deployed configuration; and the dependency graph ( : clean; semgrep: nine findings, all false positives or test fixtures). Severity Found Fixed : : High 5 5 Medium 5 5 Low 6 6 Every finding and its fix is written up in under 0.13.0. In short: Finding CWE H1 Pre MFA token accepted as a session token — MFA bypass for platform admins and owners 287 H2 All per route rate limits inert ( vs named throttlers); no account lockout despite configurable policy 307 H3 authenticated by RustDesk ID alone — plaintext rotation disclosure, password overwrite, metadata spoofing, unbounded row creation 306 H4 unrestricted — API compromise escalates to root 269 H5 Launcher trusted from the deep link — token exfiltration and hostile RustDesk reconfiguration 601/522 M1 rustdesk server .debs installed as root with no integrity check 494 M2 Launcher tokens and connection grants not atomically single use 367 M3 Open redirect via after sign in 601 M4 prefix exempted from the web auth gate 288 M5 Logo upload wrote the file before the platform admin check 285 L1–L6 Launcher tokens stored in plaintext; accounts could complete login; tenant less tokens kept frozen claims; 16 byte GCM nonce; installer accepted a non https script URL; stale in the dev store — Verified sound in this pass, and worth recording: business isolation through on every scoped read; JWT claims re read from the database on every request; argon2id passwords; CSPRNG tokens stored hashed; AES 256 GCM with the tag verified and a boot time key check; no SQL injection (two , both parameterised); no and no shell — every uses a fixed argv with ; anchored allowlists on every value interpolated into an installer script; and a deployed configuration with set, the in app updater off, loopback only proxy trust and a root owned environment file."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Summary",
        "id": "summary",
        "text": "Severity Found Fixed in this pass Remaining : : : Critical 4 4 0 High 6 6 0 Medium 5 5 0 Low 3 2 1 Regression coverage: locks in the tenant isolation, password leak, and rustdesk node scoping fixes. Run with against a dev DB."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Fixed vulnerabilities",
        "id": "fixed-vulnerabilities",
        "text": "CRITICAL — Notes: cross tenant comment bypass Files : , CWE : 639 (Authorization Bypass Through User Controlled Key / IDOR) Exploit : looked up the note by ID only ( ). Any authenticated tenant with the permission could write comments onto notes belonging to any other tenant simply by supplying the note ID. Fix : controller now passes ; the service uses and audits the comment with . Regression test : asserts that the cross tenant returns . CRITICAL — Endpoints: RustDesk password ciphertext leaked in responses Files : (findOne), and the browser side references in . CWE : 200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information) Exploit : returned the model with embedded in the response body. If the were compromised (or later weakened), all endpoint passwords could be decrypted from any historical HTTP capture. Fix : introduced helper on the service. All list and detail queries strip the ciphertext into a flag. The plaintext password is only reachable through the dedicated audited route , which now requires MFA verified sessions, is throttled, and emits . (Updated 0.30.1.) The same release also happens in the Connect script ( ) and to the desktop launcher when it redeems a single use grant. Both now need the same step up and MFA check as the route above, and are recorded as before the password leaves; itself never returns it. Regression test : asserts the response has and no field. CRITICAL — ENCRYPTION KEY silently defaulted to 32 zero bytes Files : , . CWE : 321 (Use of Hard coded Cryptographic Key) Exploit : three services fell back to if the env var was missing, producing predictable ciphertext across installations. Fix : is now validated at boot (must be 64 hex chars and not all zeros) via zod schema. Each service also refuses to start if the key is missing at construction time. CRITICAL — Update service blindly checked out unsigned GitHub tags with sudo Files : CWE : 494 (Download of Code Without Integrity Check) Exploit : fetched an arbitrary version string from the GitHub releases API and passed it directly to , then , then , then . A compromised or spoofed GitHub response could inject shell metacharacters via the tag name, and any push to a matching tag would result in root code execution. Fix : In app updater now gated by ; refuses to run otherwise, with a clear operator message. Version string is regex validated (semver only) before any shell. Downgrade rejected. runs before checkout — refuses to build if the tag is unsigned or the signature is not in the server's GPG keyring. All shell calls converted from to . HIGH — RustdeskNode queries not scoped by tenantId Files : CWE : 639 (IDOR — insufficient defense in depth) Fix : , , and now include in the where clause in addition to the pre check. also refuses to touch a node whose stored differs from the caller's. HIGH — JWT trusted stale role / platform admin / customerId claims File : CWE : 613 (Insufficient Session Expiration) / 285 Exploit : JWTs have an 8 hour TTL. Role changes, revocation of a membership, or removal of platform admin were not honored until the token expired. Fix : now re reads , verifies the tenant exists and is active, and requires an active row (unless the user is a platform admin). Role and customerId in the request scoped come from the current database row, not the frozen JWT claim. HIGH — Sudoers granted arbitrary package installation and caddy config File : (deployed live), CWE : 269 (Improper Privilege Management) Exploit : and wildcards let a compromised API process install arbitrary packages and reload caddy with any config file path. Fix : sudoers now allowlists the exact command lines the API runs ( , , , ). Live file replaced with after validation; installer script's block updated to match. HIGH — Public installer scripts interpolated tenant settings unescaped File : (buildWindowsScript, buildLinuxScript, buildMacosScript) CWE : 78 (OS Command Injection) Exploit : , , and the enrollment token were interpolated straight into single quoted PowerShell / bash"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Remaining / deferred",
        "id": "remaining-deferred",
        "text": "LOW — The implicit allow when no decorator is present is a footgun but not a live exposure. Every controller currently declares its permission explicitly. Consider deny by default in a follow up. GeoIP policy engine + world map UI — requested by the operator but not implemented in this pass. The existing table has stubs ( , ) but no enforcement code. The full requested feature — global∩customer policy inheritance, IPv4+IPv6 lookup, interactive map, per scope policy — is a multi week project. Trusted proxy work (above) is the correct precondition and is done. Ephemeral RustDesk connection grants — architecture is described in . Not implemented; requires either RustDesk credential rotation on demand or a broker service, which is out of scope here. Full jest+supertest suite — added a standalone Prisma level regression script instead. A proper Nest testing bootstrap remains to be added. Signed release manifest / SBOM / Authenticode / Apple notarization — architecture prepared (see ), external certificates required."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Verification",
        "id": "verification",
        "text": ""
      }
